Relevant columns and their potential usage  

• PLANET_FEATURES."feature_type": filter to ‘multipolygons’ (administrative areas are usually stored this way).  
• PLANET_FEATURES."all_tags": JSON‑like array – flatten to find objects where "key" = 'boundary' and "value" = 'administrative'.  
• PLANET_FEATURES."geometry": WKB geometry of the boundary – convert with TO_GEOGRAPHY() for spatial tests.  
• PLANET_FEATURES."osm_id": unique Relation/Way id we must finally return.  

• PLANET_NODES."all_tags": flatten to keep nodes whose tag object has "key" = 'amenity' (amenity POIs).  
• PLANET_NODES."latitude", PLANET_NODES."longitude": build GEOGRAPHY point with ST_MAKEPOINT() so spatial containment tests can be run.  
• PLANET_NODES."id": node id – only used for counting.  

Typical conditions  

1. Administrative boundary  
   WHERE pf_tag.value:"key"::STRING = 'boundary'  
     AND pf_tag.value:"value"::STRING = 'administrative'  
     AND pf."feature_type" = 'multipolygons'  

2. Amenity POI  
   WHERE n_tag.value:"key"::STRING = 'amenity'  

3. Spatial join  
   ST_CONTAINS( TO_GEOGRAPHY(pf."geometry") , ST_MAKEPOINT(n."longitude", n."latitude") )  

--------------------------------------------------------------------
Ten example Snowflake SQL queries (run each separately – each shows ≤20 rows)  
--------------------------------------------------------------------

```sql
